The most likely buggy method is:

`com.google.javascript.jscomp.TypeCheck.visitNew(com.google.javascript.jscomp.NodeTraversal, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node)`

Why:
- The crash happens at `FunctionType.getInstanceType()`, which throws when called on a function type without an instance type.
- In `visitNew`, the code does:

```java
JSType type = getJSType(constructor).restrictByNotNullOrUndefined();
if (type.isConstructor() || type.isEmptyType() || type.isUnknownType()) {
  FunctionType fnType = type.toMaybeFunctionType();
  if (fnType != null) {
    visitParameterList(t, n, fnType);
    ensureTyped(t, n, fnType.getInstanceType());
  }
}
```

- This is unsafe. The branch condition allows more than true constructor types (`empty` and `unknown` too), but once `fnType != null`, it unconditionally calls `fnType.getInstanceType()`.
- `getInstanceType()` requires `hasInstanceType()`, which is only true for constructors/interfaces. So `visitNew` can pass a non-constructor `FunctionType` into `getInstanceType()` and trigger the `IllegalStateException`.

Why this fits the failing test:
- The test compares two constructor functions `A === B` inside control flow.
- That kind of narrowing/merging can produce a function type that is still a `FunctionType`, but not one guaranteed to have an instance type.
- Then `new B()` reaches `visitNew`, and the unconditional `getInstanceType()` blows up.

So the root cause is a missing guard in `TypeCheck.visitNew`: it should verify that `fnType` actually has an instance type before calling `getInstanceType()`.

A secondary symptom location is:
`com.google.javascript.rhino.jstype.FunctionType.getInstanceType()`

but that method is behaving as designed; it is exposing the bad assumption made by `visitNew`.

